{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"curl -L -s 'https://clinicalgenome.org/affiliation/40069/' | grep -i -n 'TSEN2\\\\|thrombotic\\\\|microangiopathy\\\\|neurodevelopmental' | head -50\"","aggregated_output":"671:                <p>The broad scope of the Complement-Mediated Kidney Diseases Gene Curation Expert Panel (GCEP) will be to curate genes associated with complement-mediated kidney diseases, with an initial focus on thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA), particularly atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome (aHUS), and C3 glomerulopathy (C3G). Genetic forms of TMA that do not implicate the complement system (such as those due to defects in coagulation pathway genes) will also be an area of focus. To date, defects in ~40 genes have been asserted to cause these diseases and this list will continue to expand. The Complement-Mediated Kidney Diseases GCEP will first curate complement-mediated disorders that result in a primarily renal phenotype. Genes with a primary phenotype outside the scope of our work will be left until the end of phase 1 curations, with the expectation that they will only be curated if no other GCEP plans to curate them. The Complement-Mediated Kidney Diseases GCEP will also work closely with the Immunology CDWG, with representatives included in the GCEP membership. Genes of interest that overlap with existing GCEPs (e.g. Hemostasis/Thrombosis GCEP or Glomerulopathy GCEPs) will be identified and resolved, and where significant overlap exists with the purview of other GCEPs, curations will be jointly reviewed. The gene list for curation was selected based on expert consensus, combined with review of literature and of multi-gene sequencing panels from the Genetic Testing Registry.</p>        \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
